{"cross_mech":[],"family":{"alphafold_models":2696,"candidate_reasons":["pfam_previous_unknown_name"],"characterization_status":"","context_evidence_count":null,"cross_mech":{"example_proteins":0,"protein_traits_record":false,"records_by_mech":{},"scanned":false},"curation_status":"SEEDED","demotion_reasons":[],"description":"This domain is about 150 amino acids in length found associated with [pfam:PF00595] in eukaryotes. It has a conserved GILD sequence motif. Family members have been found to be essential for cell polarity establishment and maintenance such as Par3 (partitioning defective) [swissprot:Q8TEW0] and involved in conversion of histidine into ammonia (a crucial step for forming histamine in humans) such as Histidine ammonia lyase (HAL) [swissprot:P42357]. This N-terminal domain is found to mediate oligomerization critical for the membrane localization of Par-3. It is also found to possess a self-association capacity via a front-to-back mode in Par-3 and HAL proteins. However, unlike the Par-3 N-terminal domain which self-assembles into a left-handed helical filament, the HAL N-terminal domain does not tend to form a helical filament but rather self-assembles into circular oligomeric particles. This has been suggested to be likely due to the absence of equivalent charged residues that are essential for the longitudinal packing of the Par-3 N-terminal domain filament [[cite:PUB00085056]].","domain_architectures":54,"interpro_id":"IPR021922","known_evidence_count":null,"matches":5617,"name":"N-terminal of Par3 and HAL proteins","partial_evidence_count":null,"pfam_id":"PF12053","proteins":5578,"proteomes":1060,"short_name":"Par3_HAL_N_term","source_url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/api/entry/pfam/PF12053","structures":3,"taxa":3712,"unknown_status":"EX_DUF"},"inputs":{"cross_mech":"cross-mech-duf-examples-2026-10-10","members":"pfam-uniprot-uniref90-2026-10-07","protein_examples":"duf-protein-examples-direct-2026-10-13","worklist":"interpro-pfam-duf-2026